{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_b22fe622bf42a212","type":"tool_result","content":"1:---\n6:---\n201:* Terminal-Bench 2.1 (Terminus-2): We evaluate Terminal-Bench 2.1 using the Harbor/Terminus-2 framework with parser=json, temperature=1.0, top_p=1.0, and a 128K context window. Each run uses a 4-hour timeout with 32 CPU cores and 48GB RAM, and results are averaged over 5 runs. We adjust the Qwen chat template to ensure consistency between training and inference (https://huggingface.co/ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B/blob/main/chat_template.jinja), and modify Harbor to align with vLLM's reasoning_content key.<br/>\n224:<li><b>vLLM</b> ≥ 0.19.1</li>\n237:Ornith-1.5-397B is a ~397B mixture-of-experts model (≈800 GB in bf16), so multi-GPU serving is required. The recipes below use 8-way tensor parallelism on a single node (e.g., 8× H200 141GB); adjust `--tensor-parallel-size` / `--tp` to match your hardware, or use FP8/INT4 quantized builds for smaller deployments.\n239:#### vLLM\n242:vllm serve ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n243:    --served-model-name Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n244:    --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 \\\n245:    --tensor-parallel-size 8 \\\n246:    --max-model-len 262144 \\\n247:    --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 \\\n248:    --enable-prefix-caching \\\n249:    --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser qwen3_xml \\\n250:    --reasoning-parser qwen3 \\\n251:    --trust-remote-code\n257:python -m sglang.launch_server \\\n258:    --model-path ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n259:    --served-model-name Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n260:    --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 \\\n261:    --tp 8 \\\n262:    --context-length 262144 \\\n263:    --mem-fraction-static 0.85 \\\n264:    --tool-call-parser qwen3_coder \\\n265:    --reasoning-parser qwen3\n270:Ornith-1.5-397B handles context windows of up to 262,144 tokens. When a task's combined input and output must go beyond this limit, we suggest extending the effective window with RoPE scaling — YaRN is the technique we validate against, and it is already built into both vLLM and SGLang. With a scaling factor of 4.0, the usable window grows to roughly 1M tokens.\n286:- **Override at launch time.** Leave the checkpoint untouched and extend the serve commands above with the equivalent flags.\n288:  vLLM:\n291:  VLLM_ALLOW_LONG_MAX_MODEL_LEN=1 vllm serve ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B ... --hf-overrides '{\"rope_scaling\": {\"rope_type\": \"yarn\", \"factor\": 4.0, \"original_max_position_embeddings\": 262144}}' --max-model-len 1000000\n297:  SGLANG_ALLOW_OVERWRITE_LONGER_CONTEXT_LEN=1 python -m sglang.launch_server ... --json-model-override-args '{\"rope_scaling\": {\"rope_type\": \"yarn\", \"factor\": 4.0, \"original_max_position_embeddings\": 262144}}' --context-length 1000000\n307:Once a vLLM or SGLang server is running, talk to it with any OpenAI-compatible client.\n316:    api_key=\"[REDACTED]\",  # any non-empty string works for a local server\n335:You can also stream tokens, or hand the model tools — Ornith-1.5-397B emits well-formed function calls that the server parses into the standard `tool_calls` field:\n386:llama-server -hf ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B-GGUF --port 8000 -c 262144\n391:# llama.cpp — serve an OpenAI-compatible API on port 8000.\n392:llama-server -hf ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B-GGUF --port 8000 -c 262144\n397:# Hermes talks to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint — point it at your Ornith server.\n406:# OpenClaw talks to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint — point it at your Ornith server.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b6ae9cf-5f72-4206-979f-afe7dc600f73","uuid":"859a62aa-c501-4a80-a247-b095a8adb773","timestamp":"2026-09-15T16:25:26.498Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:---\n6:---\n201:* Terminal-Bench 2.1 (Terminus-2): We evaluate Terminal-Bench 2.1 using the Harbor/Terminus-2 framework with parser=json, temperature=1.0, top_p=1.0, and a 128K context window. Each run uses a 4-hour timeout with 32 CPU cores and 48GB RAM, and results are averaged over 5 runs. We adjust the Qwen chat template to ensure consistency between training and inference (https://huggingface.co/ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B/blob/main/chat_template.jinja), and modify Harbor to align with vLLM's reasoning_content key.<br/>\n224:<li><b>vLLM</b> ≥ 0.19.1</li>\n237:Ornith-1.5-397B is a ~397B mixture-of-experts model (≈800 GB in bf16), so multi-GPU serving is required. The recipes below use 8-way tensor parallelism on a single node (e.g., 8× H200 141GB); adjust `--tensor-parallel-size` / `--tp` to match your hardware, or use FP8/INT4 quantized builds for smaller deployments.\n239:#### vLLM\n242:vllm serve ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n243:    --served-model-name Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n244:    --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 \\\n245:    --tensor-parallel-size 8 \\\n246:    --max-model-len 262144 \\\n247:    --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 \\\n248:    --enable-prefix-caching \\\n249:    --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser qwen3_xml \\\n250:    --reasoning-parser qwen3 \\\n251:    --trust-remote-code\n257:python -m sglang.launch_server \\\n258:    --model-path ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n259:    --served-model-name Ornith-1.5-397B \\\n260:    --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 \\\n261:    --tp 8 \\\n262:    --context-length 262144 \\\n263:    --mem-fraction-static 0.85 \\\n264:    --tool-call-parser qwen3_coder \\\n265:    --reasoning-parser qwen3\n270:Ornith-1.5-397B handles context windows of up to 262,144 tokens. When a task's combined input and output must go beyond this limit, we suggest extending the effective window with RoPE scaling — YaRN is the technique we validate against, and it is already built into both vLLM and SGLang. With a scaling factor of 4.0, the usable window grows to roughly 1M tokens.\n286:- **Override at launch time.** Leave the checkpoint untouched and extend the serve commands above with the equivalent flags.\n288:  vLLM:\n291:  VLLM_ALLOW_LONG_MAX_MODEL_LEN=1 vllm serve ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-397B ... --hf-overrides '{\"